/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Service loads are the loads expected in normal use: dead load wD from the member, finishes and walls, and live load wL from people, furniture and stored goods.
   - wu is the ultimate load per metre, γf the load factor (for example 1.5), and wD, wL the service dead and live loads per metre, all in kN/m.
   - Different factors can be put on dead and live load, because live load is less certain, but the idea is the same: the whole margin of safety sits on the load side.
   - The ultimate moment of resistance Mu is the largest moment the section can carry before it fails.
   - For a rectangular section use the idealised concrete curve without the material factor: a parabola to a strain of 0.002 and a flat top at 0.67fck to 0.0035.
   - b is the width and d the effective depth in mm, Ast the tension steel area in mm², xu the depth of the neutral axis at failure in mm.
   - The section is safe when Mu ≥ Mu,req.
   - Limit state design kept the collapse check of ULM, split the single factor into partial safety factors on loads and on each material, and added explicit serviceability checks for deflection and cracking.
*/
