/* book Worked example: Q: A simply supported beam of span 6.0 m supports a floor with brick partitions. By calculation, its final total deflection is 22 mm and the part of the deflection that occurs after the partitions are built is 19 mm. Check both deflection limits of IS 456 and state what you would change.
   1. Span L = 6.0 m = 6000 mm.
   2. Final deflection limit = L/250 = 6000/250 = 24 mm. Actual 22 mm ≤ 24 mm: passes.
   3. Limit after partitions = smaller of L/350 = 6000/350 = 17.1 mm and 20 mm, that is 17.1 mm.
   4. Actual 19 mm > 17.1 mm: this limit fails, by 1.9 mm.
   5. The beam is safe against collapse and passes the overall deflection check, yet it can crack the partitions. Increase the depth (deflection falls roughly with the cube of depth for the same load), or delay the building of the partitions so that more of the creep deflection happens before them.
   Answer: Final deflection 22 mm ≤ 24 mm passes; deflection after partitions 19 mm > 17.1 mm fails, so the beam must be stiffened even though it is strong enough. */
